    <h2>Now <span class="mono" style="margin-left: 0.5rem; font-weight: 400;">August 2026</span></h2>
    <p>A builder at Regie.ai, on the systems that move data between customers’ CRMs and their outreach. Most of my day is design and review: drawing the system, writing the plan, and reading what my AI pair writes against it.</p>

    <h2>How I work</h2>
    <p><em>Design first.</em> I draw the system before I write it — boundaries, data flow, what can fail — and put the drawing in front of someone before code exists. Most of the drawings on this site started that way.</p>
    <p><em>Test first.</em> The test describes the behaviour; then the smallest code that passes; then clean up. Fourteen months at Incubyte made this a reflex rather than a preference.</p>
    <p><em>With an AI pair.</em> Context, then an explicit plan, then review of the plan, then code, then review again, then commit. I spend my attention on the plan; the diff is where the plan shows. I wrote the loop up in <a href="/thoughts/review-the-plan-not-the-diff/">Review the plan, not the diff</a>.</p>

    <h2>On the side</h2>
    <p><a href="https://github.com/ajayguru2/tmux-logger">tmux-logger</a>, a small tool in Rust; <a href="https://github.com/ajayguru2/slow.com">slow.com</a>, a Raycast extension that is obviously not as good as fast.com; <a href="https://github.com/ajayguru2/reflect">reflect</a>, a habit tracker with an AI coach; <a href="https://github.com/ajayguru2/claude-notebook">claude-notebook</a>. Earlier: a deep-learning nanodegree, a talk on concurrency in Go, too many tic-tac-toes.</p>

